1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,099
Old court books are full of people, but only if you can read the handwriting.

2
00:00:05,149 --> 00:00:11,589
Loghi-studio helps you read, search and correct seventeenth-century manuscripts, on your own computer.

3
00:00:12,589 --> 00:00:19,029
It is built around Loghi, the open-source handwriting recognition of the KNAW Humanities Cluster.

4
00:00:19,079 --> 00:00:23,310
Open a book and you see the scan next to the text that was read.

5
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:26,254
Choose a line, and it lights up on the page.

6
00:00:27,254 --> 00:00:29,413
Search a whole collection in seconds.

7
00:00:29,463 --> 00:00:34,428
Old spellings are no problem: the search knows that a name was written in many ways,

8
00:00:34,478 --> 00:00:37,235
and that a machine sometimes misreads a letter.

9
00:00:38,235 --> 00:00:43,260
Looking for your ancestors? Give Loghi-studio a list of names, or a whole family tree.

10
00:00:43,310 --> 00:00:47,981
It searches every book in the background and shows where each person may appear.

11
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:50,998
The machine is not always right.

12
00:00:51,048 --> 00:00:56,813
In the Correct screen you see one line, cut from the scan and shown large, above its text.

13
00:00:56,863 --> 00:00:59,914
Fix it, press Enter, and the next line is ready.

14
00:00:59,964 --> 00:01:05,858
Every line you check is kept, and can later teach the model to read this handwriting better.

15
00:01:06,858 --> 00:01:12,384
Everything works with the keyboard alone, with large text, high contrast and a screen reader.

16
00:01:12,434 --> 00:01:16,760
It was designed that way from the start, by someone who needs it himself.

17
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:23,039
Loghi-studio is free and open source, in English, Dutch and Frisian.

18
00:01:23,089 --> 00:01:27,428
You can find it on GitHub: stiensnet-beep, loghi-studio.

